1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back to the VentureX series!

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,000
In the last video, we deployed on shared hosting with cPanel —
simple, but sometimes you need more.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:12,000
More control, more performance, more flexibility.

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:14,500
That's where a VPS or cloud server comes in.

5
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,000
In this video, I'm going to set up a fresh Ubuntu server,
install everything VentureX needs,

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,000
deploy the application, and configure it for production.

7
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:26,000
I'm using DigitalOcean here, but this works on
AWS EC2, Linode, Vultr — any Ubuntu VPS.

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,000
Let's spin up a server.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
In your cloud provider's dashboard,
create a new server.

10
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:36,000
For the OS, pick Ubuntu 22.04 LTS or 24.04 LTS —
both work great with VentureX.

11
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,000
For plan size, the basic $12/month plan
with 2 gigs of RAM and one CPU is enough to get started.

12
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:43,000
You can always scale up later.

13
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,000
Choose a data center region close to your users.

14
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,000
Set your root password — make it strong, write it down.

15
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,000
Give your server a hostname like venturex-prod.

16
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,000
Click Create Droplet.

17
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:57,000
Wait about 60 seconds for it to spin up.

18
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,000
Once it's ready, copy the IP address.

19
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,000
We're going to connect to it now.

20
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Open your terminal — or use PowerShell on Windows,
or Terminal on Mac.

21
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,000
Type: ssh root@your_server_ip.

22
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,000
Accept the fingerprint, enter your password. You're in!

23
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,000
First thing — let's update the server.

24
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,000
Run: apt update && apt upgrade -y.

25
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,000
This pulls in all the latest security patches. Takes a minute.

26
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,000
Next, let's create a non-root user for security.
Run: adduser venturex.

27
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,000
Set a password, fill in the details
or just press Enter through them.

28
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,000
Now give this user sudo privileges:
usermod -aG sudo venturex.

29
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,000
Then set up the firewall — we'll use UFW.

30
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:42,000
Run: ufw allow OpenSSH,
then ufw allow 'Nginx Full', then ufw enable.

31
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,000
Say yes.

32
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,000
Now switch to your new user: su - venturex.

33
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,000
From here on, we're working as the venturex user, not root. Much safer.

34
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Now let's install PHP.

35
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,000
We need PHP 8.3 with a bunch of extensions.

36
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:04,000
First, add the PHP repository:
sudo add-apt-repository ppa:ondrej/php -y.

37
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,000
Then update again: sudo apt update.

38
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,000
Now install PHP and the extensions VentureX needs:
sudo apt install php8.3 php8.3-fpm php8.3-mysql

39
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:17,000
php8.3-curl php8.3-gd php8.3-mbstring
php8.3-xml php8.3-zip php8.3-bcmath php8.3-intl

40
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,000
php8.3-readline php8.3-opcache -y.

41
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:24,000
That's a lot of packages, but they're all important —
curl for API calls, mbstring for text handling, opcache for performance.

42
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,000
Verify it worked: php -v.

43
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,000
You should see PHP 8.3.something. Nice.

44
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Next up, MySQL.

45
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,000
Run: sudo apt install mysql-server -y.

46
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:41,000
Once it's installed, run the security script:
sudo mysql_secure_installation.

47
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:44,000
It'll ask you a few questions.

48
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:48,000
Set the VALIDATE PASSWORD component —
I choose MEDIUM for a good balance.

49
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,000
Set a strong root password.

50
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,000
Remove anonymous users? Yes.

51
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,000
Disallow root login remotely? Yes.

52
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:56,000
Remove the test database? Yes.

53
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:58,000
Reload privilege tables? Yes.

54
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:02,000
Now let's create the database and user for VentureX.
Log into MySQL: sudo mysql.

55
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,000
You're at the MySQL prompt now.

56
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:08,000
Run these three commands:
CREATE DATABASE venturex_prod;

57
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:12,000
CREATE USER 'vxuser'@'localhost'
IDENTIFIED BY 'your_strong_password';

58
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,000
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON venturex_prod.*
TO 'vxuser'@'localhost';

59
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:19,000
FLUSH PRIVILEGES; EXIT;

60
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,000
Database is ready.

61
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Now Nginx — this is our web server.

62
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,000
Install it: sudo apt install nginx -y.

63
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:32,000
Start it and enable it on boot:
sudo systemctl start nginx && sudo systemctl enable nginx.

64
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:36,000
Test it — open your browser and go to your server's IP.

65
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:39,000
You should see the default Nginx welcome page. If you do, Nginx is working.

66
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:43,000
Now let's configure it for VentureX.

67
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,000
Create a new config file:
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/venturex.

68
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:51,000
Paste in this server block — I'll link the config in the description.

69
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:55,000
The key parts are: server_name your_domain.com,
root pointing to /home/venturex/public_html/public,

70
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:59,000
and the location block handling PHP-FPM
with the try_files directive for Laravel routing.

71
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,000
Save the file with Ctrl+O, Ctrl+X.

72
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:06,000
Enable it: sudo ln -s
/etc/nginx/sites-available/venturex /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/.

73
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:10,000
Remove the default:
sudo rm /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default.

74
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:13,000
Test the config: sudo nginx -t.

75
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:17,000
If it says syntax is ok and test is successful,
reload Nginx: sudo systemctl reload nginx.

76
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Time to get VentureX on the server.

77
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:24,000
Two options here.

78
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:28,000
Option one — Git.
If your code is in a Git repository:
cd /home/venturex,

79
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,000
then git clone your-repo-url public_html.

80
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:35,000
It clones everything into the public_html folder.

81
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,000
Option two — SCP from your local machine.

82
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:43,000
Open a new terminal on your computer:
scp -r /path/to/venturex/*

83
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:47,000
venturex@your_server_ip:/home/venturex/public_html.

84
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:50,000
Enter your password, and it copies everything over.

85
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:54,000
Once the files are there, set permissions:
sudo chown -R venturex:venturex /home/venturex/public_html.

86
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,000
Then: cd public_html.

87
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:00,000
Copy the environment file: cp .env.example .env.

88
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:03,000
Edit it: nano .env.

89
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:07,000
Fill in your database details —
DB_HOST is 127.0.0.1, DB_DATABASE is venturex_prod,

90
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:10,000
DB_USERNAME is vxuser, DB_PASSWORD is your password.

91
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:13,000
Set APP_URL to https://yourdomain.com.

92
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,000
Save and exit.

93
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,000
Generate the key: php artisan key:generate.

94
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:22,000
Install dependencies:
composer install --no-dev --optimize-autoloader.

95
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:26,000
Set permissions again:
chmod -R 755 storage bootstrap/cache.

96
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:29,000
Create the storage link: php artisan storage:link.

97
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Free SSL with Certbot.

98
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:37,000
First, make sure your domain's DNS is pointing to your server IP —
A record pointing to the IP address.

99
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,000
Once DNS has propagated, install Certbot:
sudo apt install certbot python3-certbot-nginx -y.

100
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:45,000
Run it: sudo certbot --nginx
-d yourdomain.com -d www.yourdomain.com.

101
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:49,000
It'll ask for your email, agree to terms,
and ask if you want to redirect HTTP to HTTPS — choose yes.

102
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:52,000
Certbot automatically configures Nginx for you.

103
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:55,000
Verify: sudo certbot certificates.

104
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:58,000
You'll see your domain with a valid certificate.

105
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:01,000
Auto-renewal is set up by default.

106
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:04,000
Test it: sudo certbot renew --dry-run.

107
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:07,000
If no errors, you're good.

108
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:10,000
Your site is now live with HTTPS.

109
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
VentureX uses queues for background jobs —
things like sending bulk emails,

110
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:18,000
processing AI requests, and generating reports.

111
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,000
We need Supervisor to keep those workers running.

112
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:25,000
Install it: sudo apt install supervisor -y.

113
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:29,000
Create a worker config:
sudo nano /etc/supervisor/conf.d/venturex-worker.conf.

114
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:33,000
Paste this in — a program block with the command
php artisan queue:work, user venturex,

115
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:36,000
autostart and autorestart enabled.

116
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:38,000
Save and exit.

117
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:41,000
Reload Supervisor: sudo supervisorctl reread,

118
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:44,000
then sudo supervisorctl update,

119
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:48,000
then sudo supervisorctl start venturex-worker:*.

120
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:51,000
Check status: sudo supervisorctl status.

121
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:54,000
You should see your worker running.

122
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:57,000
If it says FATAL, check the log file for errors.

123
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Let's squeeze some extra performance out.

124
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:05,000
First, PHP OPcache — check it's enabled:
php -m | grep opcache.

125
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:07,000
It should show up.

126
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:11,000
Then edit PHP config:
sudo nano /etc/php/8.3/fpm/php.ini.

127
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:15,000
Find these settings and adjust them:
opcache.enable=1, opcache.memory_consumption=128,

128
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:19,000
opcache.max_accelerated_files=10000,
opcache.revalidate_freq=2.

129
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,000
Save.

130
00:07:21,200 --> 00:07:25,000
Restart PHP-FPM:
sudo systemctl restart php8.3-fpm.

131
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:29,000
Next, Nginx caching. Add these lines to your server block:
location ~* \.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif|ico|css|js)$

132
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:33,000
{ expires 30d; add_header Cache-Control "public, immutable"; }

133
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:37,000
This tells the browser to cache static files for 30 days.

134
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:39,000
Reload Nginx.

135
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:43,000
Finally, MySQL tuning:
sudo nano /etc/mysql/mysql.conf.d/mysqld.cnf.

136
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:47,000
For a 2GB RAM server,
set innodb_buffer_pool_size to 256M

137
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:50,000
and query_cache_size to 32M.

138
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:53,000
Restart MySQL: sudo systemctl restart mysql.

139
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:57,000
These changes alone can make your site feel twice as fast.

140
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
And that's it — VentureX is live on your own VPS!

141
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:05,000
You've got full control, full performance,
and you're not sharing resources with anyone.

142
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,000
Open your browser, go to your domain, and log in.

143
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:12,000
Everything should work exactly like it did locally,
but now it's production-ready.

144
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:16,000
A few tips going forward:
set up automatic backups with a script and cron job,

145
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:20,000
monitor your server with something like Netdata or UptimeRobot,

146
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:24,000
and keep your system updated regularly
with sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade.

147
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:28,000
In the next video, we'll cover WordPress integration —
how to connect your WordPress site directly to VentureX.

148
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:30,000
See you there!
